within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.A_AlimentaryTractAndMetabolism.A10B_BloodGlucoseLoweringDrugsExclInsulins.A10BA02_Metformin;
model Metformin_Hong2008_PD_fla
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Hong_2008 :: FLA (fasting lactic acid)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Interfaces.PartialExposureResponseSweep(
    E0 = 1.3,      // [mM]
    Emax = 0, EC50 = 1,      // unused by a line
    exposure_per_s = 1e-06);
  parameter Real slope = 0.5   "[meter ** 4 / kilogram; mM⋅mL⋅ng -1 × 1000]";
equation
  response = E0 + slope*exposure;
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Metformin_Hong2008_PD_fla</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Hong Y et al., <i>Journal of clinical pharmacology 48(6):696–707</i> 2008 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0091270008316884\">doi:10.1177/0091270008316884</a>) — fasting lactic acid [mM] against metformin ng·mL -1 — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =        1.3 mM       drug-free response (baseline) (Baseline fasting lactic acid, Q324)
  slope =     0.0005 mM⋅mL⋅ng -1 change in response per unit of exposure (Slope coefficient, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <code>response = E0 + slope*exposure</code>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one ng·mL -1 of metformin per second of simulated time (exposure is in kg/m3): there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 1.3   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Hong_2008: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: curated candidate</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Metformin_Hong2008_PD_fla;
